>> title: Onion Bhaji
>> category: Flatbreads & Pancakes
>> tags: indian, onion, gram flour, fried, starter
>> counters: Curry House
>> aka: onion bhajee, bhaji, pyaz pakora, onion pakora, kanda bhaji, bhajia
>> pairs-with: mint-chutney, papadom
>> servings: 12
>> time: 45 min
>> slack: narrow — the onions are salted until they weep and no water is added, because the batter has to be barely holding: any looser and it fries into a solid fritter with raw onion inside, and there is no correcting it once the batch is in the oil
>> step.1: salt and stand 20 min, until they weep
>> step.2: toss through, no water yet
>> step.3: fry 5 min a batch, at 340°F

Rest @yellow onions{1%lb}(450 g; halved and sliced thin) under @kosher salt{1 1/2%tsp} ~stand{20%min}, turning them once, until the bowl has an inch of water in the bottom and the slices have gone floppy.

Toss @gram flour{3/4%cup}(80 g; besan), @rice flour{2%Tbs}(20 g), @ground turmeric{1/2%tsp}, @Kashmiri chile powder{1%tsp}, @cumin seeds{1%tsp}, @ajwain{1/2%tsp}, @fresh ginger{1%Tbs}(grated) and @cilantro{3%Tbs}(chopped) through @&(~1)wept onions{}, water and all, and work it with your hand until every strand is coated in a thick paste. Add no water. The onions have already given you exactly enough, and a batter you can pour makes fritters that are heavy in the middle.

Fry @&(~1)coated onions{} in @neutral oil{4%cup}(950 mL) at 340°F (170°C) ~fry{5%min} a batch, dropped in craggy tablespoonfuls and turned once, until deep gold and crisp at every loose end.

Gram flour is not a substitute for anything here — it fries crisp where wheat flour goes soft, and it is why this tastes of a curry house rather than a chip shop. Pakora is the same batter around something else: potato slices, cauliflower florets, whole green chiles, spinach leaves. Rice flour is the shop trick for a shell that is still crisp when the plate reaches the table. These are the first deep-fried things on this site and they want to be eaten standing up.
